<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sampsigeramus I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sampsigeramus I was an Arab chieftain who founded the priest-kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, modern Homs, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, controlling the Orontes valley and the town of Arethusa as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> collapsed in the 1st century BCE. He submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in 64 BCE and was recognised as a client ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His descendants, the Emesene dynasty, ruled until the 70s CE.</p></div>
